# How many backtests survive honest testing?

*A study of selection bias in systematic trading-strategy research.*

**Data used for the empirical results (E3 to E5): {{e3.data.label}}.** Experiments E1 and E2 are synthetic by design. Every number and table in this report is written by the pipeline from `reports/results/*.json`. The prose that interprets them was written for the committed run; if you re-run on other data, re-read it against the regenerated tables.

## Abstract

We generate {{e3.n_variants:,}} variants of five classic technical trading rules on {{e3.n_assets}} crypto assets, backtest them net of costs on {{e3.net.years:.1f}} years of daily data, and ask how many remain statistically convincing once the search itself is accounted for. Tested one at a time at the 5% level, {{e3.net.survivors.naive:,}} variants ({{e3.net.survivors.naive_frac:.1%}}) look significant. After Holm's correction {{e3.net.survivors.holm:,}} remain; after Benjamini-Hochberg {{e3.net.survivors.bh:,}}. The best variant's annualised Sharpe ratio of {{e3.net.sharpe.best_annual:.2f}} is {{e3.verdict.dsr_text}} what the best of that many skill-less strategies is expected to show ({{e3.net.dsr_primary.sr0_annual:.2f}}), giving a Deflated Sharpe Ratio of {{e3.net.dsr_primary.dsr:.2f}}. Hansen's SPA test {{e3.verdict.spa_text}} the hypothesis that no variant has positive expected return (p = {{e3.net.snooping.spa_p:.2f}}), and the Probability of Backtest Overfitting is {{e3.net.pbo.pbo:.2f}}. Measured against buy-and-hold of the same coin instead of cash, {{e3.vs_buy_hold.survivors.naive:,}} variants are significant even before correction. The same tools are first validated on synthetic markets with no signal, where they should stay silent, and on markets with a planted signal, where they should not. That validation exposed two finite-sample problems in textbook recipes (section 5.1). Both were corrected on the evidence of the synthetic worlds alone, and the real-data results are shown with the original and the corrected versions side by side.

## 1. Introduction

A backtest answers the question "how would this rule have done?" A research process asks that question thousands of times and reports the best answer. The best of many noisy estimates is biased upwards even when every underlying strategy is worthless, so the reported Sharpe ratio of a selected strategy overstates what an investor should expect. Bailey, Borwein, Lopez de Prado and Zhu (2014) call the result "pseudo-mathematics": with enough trials, any Sharpe ratio can be manufactured from noise.

This project measures the size of that effect in a controlled setting. It has three parts:

1. a transparent strategy factory and backtester that produce one T x N matrix of daily returns (section 3);
2. implementations, from the original papers, of the main diagnostics for selection bias (section 4);
3. five experiments (section 5): a null world (E1), a power study (E2), real data (E3), cost sensitivity (E4) and robustness (E5).

The contribution is not a trading strategy. It is a worked demonstration, with tests, of how much of a "best backtest" survives when the search is counted honestly, and of how the diagnostic tools themselves behave when the truth is known.

