{{e1.tables.mc_md}}

![E1 false-positive rates](reports/figures/e1_false_positive_rates.png)

Reading the table:

- Tested one at a time, {{e1.summary.naive_frac_min:.1%}} to {{e1.summary.naive_frac_max:.1%}} of variants are significant at 5%, as they should be, which means that almost every world contains "discoveries".
- Holm's family-wise error rate is at most {{e1.summary.max_fwer_holm:.1%}} and Benjamini-Hochberg makes any discovery in at most {{e1.summary.max_fdr_bh:.1%}} of worlds. The Reality Check rejects in at most {{e1.summary.max_rc:.1%}}, SPA in at most {{e1.summary.max_spa:.1%}}, and the DSR passes in at most {{e1.summary.max_dsr:.1%}} ({{e1.summary.max_dsr_null:.1%}} with the theoretical dispersion discussed in section 5.2).
- Mean PBO across null worlds is between {{e1.summary.mean_pbo_min:.2f}} and {{e1.summary.mean_pbo_max:.2f}}, close to the 0.5 that pure noise should give.

**Finding 1: PSR with in-sample skewness is not safe for multiple testing.** Bonferroni applied to $`1 - PSR(0)`$ made false discoveries in up to {{e1.summary.max_bonferroni_psr:.1%}} of null worlds, although Bonferroni cannot exceed 5% when the p-values are valid. In the {{e1.summary.psr_offender_n}} worlds where this happened, the variant responsible had a non-zero return on a median of {{e1.summary.psr_offender_median:.0f}} of the {{e1.mc.gbm.n_bars:,}} bars (range {{e1.summary.psr_offender_min}} to {{e1.summary.psr_offender_max}}). For a variant that is almost never in the market a few lucky returns raise the sample mean and the sample skewness together, the term $`-\hat\gamma_3\widehat{SR}`$ shrinks the standard error, and the p-value becomes far too small. Evaluating the standard error under the null removes the problem, so that is what the corrections use (`sharpe_pvalue`). PSR itself is still reported for the selected variant and still drives the DSR.

**Finding 2: the SPA statistic is sensitive to how it is studentised.** With the long-run standard deviation, as in Hansen (2005), SPA rejected in up to {{e1.summary.max_spa_hac:.1%}} of null worlds; with the sample standard deviation the figure is {{e1.summary.max_spa:.1%}}. The long-run estimate adds many noisy sample autocovariances to the variance, and a maximum over thousands of ratios picks out the variants whose estimate happens to be too small. In the {{e1.summary.spa_hac_reject_n}} worlds where the long-run version rejected, the winning variant's long-run estimate was a median of {{e1.summary.spa_hac_ratio_median:.0%}} of its sample standard deviation. The bootstrap holds the scale fixed and cannot see this. The Monte Carlo rates carry sampling error of a few percentage points (see the intervals), so the size of the excess is uncertain, but the mechanism is reproducible (`tests/test_bootstrap.py`).

**Finding 3: counting clusters is not the same as counting independent trials.** The table compares each estimator's predicted best Sharpe ratio $`SR_0`$ with the realised average best Sharpe ratio across null worlds ({{e1.summary.realised_best_gbm:.2f}} for GBM, {{e1.summary.realised_best_garch:.2f}} for GARCH).

{{e1.tables.calibration_md}}

The raw count and the average-correlation formula overstate the luck benchmark, which makes the DSR conservative. Clustering and the eigenvalue ratio understate it badly, and a DSR built on the cluster count passed in up to {{e1.summary.max_dsr_cluster:.1%}} of null worlds. A cluster of similar strategies is not one trial: its members are correlated but not identical, so the best of them is still inflated. Max-matching comes closest and errs slightly low, which the DSR's own margin absorbs.

### 5.2 E2: power

**Setup.** GARCH markets with a planted signal of increasing strength, {{e2.n_worlds}} worlds per level, {{e2.n_variants:,}} variants and {{e2.n_bars:,}} daily bars each, net of costs. "Detection" means the method declares at least one variant, or the family, significant.

{{e2.tables.levels_md}}

{{e2.tables.thresholds_md}}

![E2 power](reports/figures/e2_power.png)

{{e2.summary.sentence}} The second column of the first table is the average net Sharpe ratio, across worlds and assets, of the best fixed variant: it shows how much of the oracle's edge simple price rules capture. It is a maximum of noisy averages, so it is biased upwards; its value at zero signal ({{e2.summary.true_best_at_zero:.2f}}, where the truth is at most zero) shows by how much. Mean PBO falls from {{e2.summary.pbo_at_zero:.2f}} with no signal to {{e2.summary.pbo_at_max:.2f}} at the strongest.

**The DSR's dispersion term.** At the strongest signal (oracle Sharpe {{e2.summary.max_strength:g}}, best variant's true Sharpe about {{e2.summary.true_best_at_max:.2f}}) SPA detects in {{e2.summary.spa_at_max:.0%}} of worlds and the DSR as defined in the paper in {{e2.summary.dsr_paper_at_max:.0%}}. The paper sets the luck benchmark from the cross-sectional variance of the trial Sharpe ratios. Under the null that variance is sampling noise, but a real signal makes trend rules good and reversal rules bad, which widens the cross-section and raises the benchmark along with the best Sharpe. Replacing the cross-sectional variance by the theoretical sampling variance $`1/(T-1)`$ removes the feedback: that variant detects in {{e2.summary.dsr_null_at_max:.0%}} of worlds at the strongest signal and passed in at most {{e1.summary.max_dsr_null:.1%}} of null worlds. The headline DSR keeps the paper's definition and the variant is reported beside it.

The practical message is the mirror image of E1: the thresholds that keep the tools quiet under the null also hide small real edges, so with this much data a strategy needs a large true Sharpe ratio before these procedures can tell it from luck. Methods that control the false-positive rate most tightly pay for it in power.

