**Reading the results.** {{e3.verdict.overall}} The best variant is `{{e3.best.label}}`. Three things stand out.

First, the gap between naive and corrected counts is the headline: {{e3.net.survivors.naive:,}} variants pass a 5% test, {{e3.verdict.bh_text}}, and {{e3.verdict.holm_text}}.

Second, the naive count is far above the 5% that pure noise would give, and PBO ({{e3.net.pbo.pbo:.2f}}) is below the 0.5 of the null worlds, so the matrix is not pure noise: {{e3.verdict.pbo_text}}. The family table shows where the structure is. Trend-type rules have positive median Sharpe ratios and mean-reversion rules do not, and the assets rose strongly over the sample (see section 2.1). A long-only trend rule on a rising asset collects much of that drift. This is a real positive expected return over cash, which is what Benjamini-Hochberg picks up, but it is market exposure rather than timing skill. The active-return column makes the point: against buy-and-hold the best Sharpe ratio is {{e3.vs_buy_hold.sharpe.best_annual:.2f}}, only {{e3.vs_buy_hold.sharpe.frac_positive:.0%}} of variants beat their own asset, {{e3.vs_buy_hold.survivors.naive:,}} are significant before any correction, and the SPA p-value is {{e3.vs_buy_hold.snooping.spa_p:.2f}}.

Third, the best Sharpe ratio is {{e3.verdict.dsr_text}} the luck benchmark, so the single best backtest carries no evidence beyond the family it came from. Whatever is real in this universe is a broad, modest exposure shared by hundreds of variants, not the standout performance of the winner.

### 5.4 E4: cost sensitivity

